#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "9ec1a675-6563-5374-85fd-871da49caf30" :question/math-academy-id "e-3727" :question/problem "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5,$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/id #uuid "3f70f692-38d3-5b7e-9bbb-9fc089679fa0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3729" :question/problem "Find the stationary points of the function $f(x) =x^3$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a82def66-255f-5658-9109-ab7966855c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3730" :question/problem "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f772e051-657c-598b-953d-aefbfeca8e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292966} {:answer/id #uuid "95862677-e4f3-545a-8efd-e2a2122f2379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292967} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c5fc8d-7493-57d2-b12a-fd3be232c6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292968} {:answer/id #uuid "631dcd5d-8feb-5ea0-879b-8b738b9f80d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292969} {:answer/id #uuid "49731ccb-509a-5c99-8306-e8a84b760082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631dcd5d-8feb-5ea0-879b-8b738b9f80d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292969} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b571ce7-3c29-5354-85c0-f9325e4fd427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b49a83d0-be9c-5ea2-af93-457dbf50bb25" :question/math-academy-id "q-113750" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=12x+9{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12+18x-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6(2{x}^{2}-3x-2) \\\\  & =-6(x-2)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-2)(2x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-6(-1-2)(2(-1)+1)=-18<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-2)(2(0)+1)=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-6(3-2)(2(3)+1)=-42<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab6412de-3305-5998-962d-82a19fb91481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292973} {:answer/id #uuid "c5de79bd-d4ad-5727-83f3-fb1c45b3528b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292974} {:answer/id #uuid "d34bc2f3-d2f5-5136-900d-dee8622b2d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292975} {:answer/id #uuid "3755b882-caad-5740-a693-c2910f9cd73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292976} {:answer/id #uuid "8be996ef-fde7-5ee9-b061-835bac1ce055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3755b882-caad-5740-a693-c2910f9cd73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292976} :answer-field/id #uuid "1149029e-4122-51cd-a2aa-0980a00ac29d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f661c07-7884-5002-810c-d6680fa001b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-113758" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29e3487-e0d5-5619-942e-66e55b9015a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303164} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3d28cc-1ab6-5f34-a650-78711ffa2294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303165} {:answer/id #uuid "06c4c9eb-f86e-5536-b095-bb6cd4e29bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303166} {:answer/id #uuid "a733457a-c441-5230-925d-a12a204900b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303167} {:answer/id #uuid "6741f1d8-0e76-5d19-ab0b-7847d8c593c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c4c9eb-f86e-5536-b095-bb6cd4e29bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303166} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a3c5888-f08b-57a9-8445-91e772da6c66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9a92603-1c58-5381-9a97-44ce967ac9d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113762" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x+5,$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}+6x-3 \\\\  & =-3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-3(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3(x-1)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0-1)^{2}=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-3(2-1)^{2}=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc58338-ffd3-5655-9cbe-3d640666a7f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303171} {:answer/id #uuid "5b33665a-1d58-5ff6-a744-5babee0c2b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303172} {:answer/id #uuid "a93f83b3-d67e-563b-be91-54bbed67199f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303173} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd8d1bb-a88c-5f25-a74e-013aa6c6fd68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303174} {:answer/id #uuid "39acc041-7f03-58cc-8d8b-a10f6b78c616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddd8d1bb-a88c-5f25-a74e-013aa6c6fd68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303174} :answer-field/id #uuid "82cece45-c39d-5eb8-a9be-12814d5d653d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d734b4ff-5976-5354-96f9-b1ca59071265" :question/math-academy-id "q-113768" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{{x}^{3}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2x-3){x}^{3}-3({x}^{2}-3x+3){x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-3{x}^{2}+9x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+6x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $x=3$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{(1-3)^{2}}{{1}^{4}}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{(4-3)^{2}}{{4}^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{256}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=3.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=3$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $(0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbd6480-4929-5949-a0b1-a153881b8a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303178} {:answer/id #uuid "827e9847-0070-52a6-95e1-7794ccecf414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303179} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b76953-7cdd-5176-9bad-4c05b0ca7cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303180} {:answer/id #uuid "66a03397-9f2f-56bd-8d80-5e198dc9d668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303181} {:answer/id #uuid "99b1c3a7-5dfa-54d7-b84a-2651e9b4aea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66a03397-9f2f-56bd-8d80-5e198dc9d668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303181} :answer-field/id #uuid "39d8d08d-8ae7-56e2-9446-ea879b820394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f13d0a0f-3535-547f-bf2f-0daa5e0b2ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-21467" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-12x+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-2$ is a local maximum\n- $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- $x=1$ is a local maximum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+6x-12 \\\\  & =6({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & =6(x+2)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x+2)(x-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =6(-3+2)(-3-1)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6(0+2)(0-1)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2+2)(2-1)=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "147d98de-aec3-5443-83a9-b7444200833f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303185} {:answer/id #uuid "c32a91b6-0aa1-5a7a-ada6-cbe1f5a62563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303186} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9a3518-974e-5c8e-af40-3c5bbcab09d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303187} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e91357-5531-50a8-802d-aceb74c63121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303188} {:answer/id #uuid "c78a7de0-8f84-52dc-a2b2-893436a724aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c32a91b6-0aa1-5a7a-ada6-cbe1f5a62563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303186} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef257078-8c2b-5897-9e3a-55eb8c36d31a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f2f830a-8fd4-5def-9efa-5e26534df70d" :question/math-academy-id "q-22504" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 2x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & ={e}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x={e}^{1/3},e$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}({e}^{1/3}) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{1/3}}{{({e}^{1/3})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{{e}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,{e}^{1/2})$ | ${e}^{1/2}$ | $({e}^{1/2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | ${e}^{1/3}$ |  | $e$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x={e}^{1/2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x={e}^{1/2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deb22cef-be44-5461-9403-99d0fb746b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deb22cef-be44-5461-9403-99d0fb746b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303192} :answer-field/id #uuid "0910c24f-a334-5163-bcc5-85d7ff3202bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02733b2a-13b7-528a-9373-3c369c69bd24" :question/math-academy-id "q-344272" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-9x-11$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}+6x-9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+6x-9=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =3(-4)^{2}+6(-4)-9=15>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}+6(0)-9=-9<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3(2)^{2}+6(2)-9=15>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46044faa-fd0f-523d-8558-4a0b6be2b490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46044faa-fd0f-523d-8558-4a0b6be2b490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303195} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c217a49-4a04-5594-9b51-ea4e207ee936" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14ac5621-765a-5a3d-9c74-f6ac403575d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-344388" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+1){e}^{x}$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+1)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x-3)\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x+1)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ and $x=3$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & ={e}^{-2}(-1)(-5)=5{e}^{-2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}(1)(-3)=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & ={e}^{4}(5)(1)=5{e}^{4}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b28d505-963c-58d1-847b-5cf99aa4bff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303198} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7ead62-956d-5576-8252-9eee787e03a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b28d505-963c-58d1-847b-5cf99aa4bff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303198} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae6b923-49f9-5959-92e9-746e4296e141" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1aa25549-7d3d-5f53-8902-eddd426eb705" :question/math-academy-id "q-344405" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{1-(-2)^{2}}{{((-2)^{2}+1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1-4}{(5)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1-{0}^{2}}{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{2}}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1-{2}^{2}}{{({2}^{2}+1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1-4}{(5)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c228c54-cb22-56c9-89e7-f009205ed1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104256} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb20ecb-3cf6-58b5-bd9d-6b965a3762f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104257} {:answer/id #uuid "c4deb98e-cd6b-547a-acfc-ec16190f06c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104258} {:answer/id #uuid "d48460d1-1d9f-59d8-ad06-074f62eacc33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104259} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d0807b-7929-566e-938d-f243ec8608a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c228c54-cb22-56c9-89e7-f009205ed1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104256} :answer-field/id #uuid "840c6519-ee5d-5b70-b43a-17d5816bc817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "273e570a-a467-5f2a-8bbc-0b66a3856ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-49022" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=(x+2)x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-2$ and $x=0$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =(-3+2)(-3){e}^{-3}=3{e}^{-3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1+2)(-1){e}^{-1}=-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1+2)(1){e}^{1}=3e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aba295c-5d9b-50b7-bdf1-35bbc62f7ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no local extrema on $x\\in [0,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303202} {:answer/id #uuid "082cfc77-14e4-56e6-b30d-af3caed2574a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum at $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303203} {:answer/id #uuid "f21e2a5c-f0a1-54b1-9978-2ddcd71f622c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum at $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303204} {:answer/id #uuid "943136fd-3665-53ba-b52e-6835f64b4c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303205} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd32917-3023-579d-b228-6de320190b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aba295c-5d9b-50b7-bdf1-35bbc62f7ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no local extrema on $x\\in [0,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303202} :answer-field/id #uuid "4001918b-d326-5595-821e-1d2487652b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac621d85-6618-59f0-8173-be7a4706164c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49025" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{3}{e}^{x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x-1)^{3}{e}^{x}+3(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x}(x-1+3) \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}(x+2){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}(x+2){e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =(0-1)^{2}(0+2){e}^{0}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =(2-1)^{2}(2+2){e}^{2}=4{e}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $[0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $[0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfce3109-00b3-5d91-8b9e-8d33a75183bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303209} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4a1e9d-c345-59a1-9a67-1560862c2b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303210} {:answer/id #uuid "868c0540-12d0-5309-83e6-3f5a41dec19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303211} {:answer/id #uuid "a4762953-66f1-57c4-a231-5fe9f0006370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303212} {:answer/id #uuid "32dbf757-0832-5821-aec0-80773ba20a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4762953-66f1-57c4-a231-5fe9f0006370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303212} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff2da374-3b73-50db-bed3-652771d01274" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee2cc97e-2362-5237-bd9e-282271c99f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49033" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{x}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x)\\cdot {e}^{x}-{e}^{x}=-x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-(1){e}^{1}=-e<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b5ed1c8-16bc-5417-a259-3f27f7688a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293024} {:answer/id #uuid "036384c0-3ff8-5c09-9941-904335813c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025} {:answer/id #uuid "0efdd25c-a9e2-55ab-a249-a953db365c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293026} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4fbafb-8a83-515e-98f4-a4ab95fcc039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293027} {:answer/id #uuid "33f4cd0b-b327-59f9-bfa1-c42341ea4659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036384c0-3ff8-5c09-9941-904335813c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec30606-b998-52be-b98d-f8b320073278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a38627ef-e4b2-52dc-9751-9805f2cf3368" :question/math-academy-id "q-49035" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-5,$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=2$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-12(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-12(3)+12=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=2.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=2$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be612e63-2e38-5877-b165-01182404ce82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303216} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae10881-e54e-5906-9ce5-d2eac7acf29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local minima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303217} {:answer/id #uuid "9a518d61-c933-5b78-b9d9-8e84118fb384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303218} {:answer/id #uuid "e0db6157-98a9-567b-b3ad-b81cceac2e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303219} {:answer/id #uuid "e88edebc-10aa-577c-9885-d56fe5e6160f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a518d61-c933-5b78-b9d9-8e84118fb384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303218} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f55c3cf-eb7e-55ec-9964-c22648d9cd49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b083dc1-b3f3-517b-a6eb-d7231518e3ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-78983" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+5$ have a local minimum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3((-2)^{2}-1)=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3((0)^{2}-1)=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3((2)^{2}-1)=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f8eee3b-af6e-5563-8933-55f19dc50656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293031} {:answer/id #uuid "7222a074-187f-59bd-a74d-5cfb174f1fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293032} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5c16c2-895e-521a-919a-e1e87c48f797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293033} {:answer/id #uuid "e5515bc2-998f-5f72-bc4c-2343a4dfee35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293034} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce8789c-445e-5549-93a6-70a74a3ce981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5515bc2-998f-5f72-bc4c-2343a4dfee35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293034} :answer-field/id #uuid "88615d9c-3523-584a-99fc-2013f6fabcd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfa42c7e-dc08-577a-b09e-d8864c39ea66" :question/math-academy-id "q-79001" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=2+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}=2{x}^{2}(3-2x).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}(3-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}(3-2(-1))=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)^{2}(3-2(1))=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)^{2}(3-2(2))=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
